WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.399
Coordination brings specialized contributions together.

2
00:00:03.599 --> 00:00:09.827
At Cedar Crossing, two people publish different workshop
times because no one confirms which schedule is final.

3
00:00:10.027 --> 00:00:13.314
Another meeting might help, but a meeting is not the
only tool.

4
00:00:13.514 --> 00:00:18.286
Mutual adjustment means people coordinate through direct
informal communication.

5
00:00:18.486 --> 00:00:22.430
Direct supervision gives someone responsibility for
directing the work.

6
00:00:22.630 --> 00:00:26.832
Standardizing work processes specifies how a recurring
task is done.

7
00:00:27.032 --> 00:00:30.313
Standardizing outputs specifies the required result.

8
00:00:30.513 --> 00:00:34.759
Standardizing skills establishes the preparation needed
to perform the work.

9
00:00:34.959 --> 00:00:38.104
These are the chapter's five coordinating mechanisms.

10
00:00:38.304 --> 00:00:40.102
Cedar Crossing can combine them.

11
00:00:40.302 --> 00:00:46.546
A shared checklist can specify that the programs lead
confirms the time before public services publishes it.

12
00:00:46.746 --> 00:00:50.613
A brief conversation can resolve an unusual equipment
request.

13
00:00:50.813 --> 00:00:55.090
The checklist needs an owner, current information, and a
route for exceptions.

14
00:00:55.290 --> 00:01:00.384
Too little coordination leaves gaps; too much consumes
the time needed to deliver the service.

15
00:01:00.584 --> 00:01:06.707
Ask whether the next person receives the right
information at the right time, not simply how many
meetings took place.

